common='''- The source calls the Wanjiru case real; its independent provenance and nutrient estimates were not supplied. It is labelled source case data, not a diagnosis. The case graph does not by itself prove which food or hormone caused the pattern. No pupils are asked to fast, test blood or disclose health information.\n- Source resource links on the hidden glance slide are provided only as unverified links; preview content for accuracy and spoilers, and verify ARES offline access. Additional source URLs are listed below.\n- The original final paper/key assumes exact observations, overstates enzyme temperature rules and treats some inferred physiological details as measured facts. The adapted student assessment requests actual observations; the teacher must review and adapt the source key and rubric.\n- Whole-unit outcomes mention vitamin C tests and investigation of enzymes in living tissues, with catalase in values, but the six-lesson activities do not clearly provide separate vitamin C or catalase investigations. Source-owner decision is open; no new practical was invented.\n- All graphics, line plots, tables and labels are editable except the contextual illustrations and institutional logos. Source files remain unchanged. Native PowerPoint/Presenter View and teacher approval remain open.\n- CPU-only: PPTX and DOCX assembly, LibreOffice PDF conversion, PDF rasterisation, image encoding, document layout and ZIP packaging. CUDA GB10 is used for raster preview resizing and pixel metrics; it is not a science or semantic review.\n'''
specific={
1:'- Source L1 Predict: the graph is not revealed until after individual prediction and class vote; first explanations remain tentative. The sampled first return into the supplied fasting range after the peak is 120 min; no claim is made about unmeasured times.\n- Meal quantities are source estimates rather than class-measured evidence; the case is not a diagnostic test. The source’s lesson timing was fitted to 80 minutes without cutting the main tasks.\n',
2:'- Source L2 Station C infers carbon from a black residue. The deck asks learners to describe actual observations and treats darkening as consistent with charring, not definitive identification by appearance. Teacher-only flame demonstration and its local risk approval are open.\n- The source’s C–H rule is described as useful for the named food groups, not a universal definition of all organic compounds. Supplied element-card details are incomplete; the teacher must provide verified examples for P, Mg, Na and K.\n',
3:'- Source L3 prescribes expected sample colours. Student records use actual outcomes; negative Benedict’s is limited by the method, and the test detects reducing sugars rather than glucose uniquely. The test guide appears only after recording.\n- Iodine, Benedict’s, hot water, materials, concentrations, allergy handling, waste, controls and accessibility need local teacher review. A bead chain represents a model, not a photograph or direct evidence of a specific digestive product.\n',
4:'- Source L4 implies a single test and meal graph can rule out other nutrients as causes. The deck treats carbohydrate as a plausible contributor and does not claim the case isolates causal effects. Protein, lipid and starch building-block comparisons are stated as selected examples, not universal rules.\n- Biuret concentrations are unspecified; ethanol is flammable and groundnuts/milk pose allergy issues. The teacher must approve reagent strengths, risk controls and disposal. Actual results may differ from source expectations.\n',
5:'- Source L5 proposes human saliva, including a pooled-saliva option. The deck flags this for school and teacher biosafety review and does not endorse pooling or invent an alternative. The source-permitted water and boiled-saliva controls are distinct comparisons; the teacher must choose and record one and its limits.\n- Corrected overgeneralised claims: salivary amylase produces smaller products including maltose, and other enzymes including maltase produce glucose; amylase is a protein but not all enzymes are proteins. Cold can slow activity without proving zero activity at 0 °C; 37 °C is not a universal optimum. No expected colours are presented as observed results.\n',
6:'- Source L6 treats a role-play as if it directly shows Wanjiru’s biology and asks counters to track exact case values. The deck labels it a qualitative model, with no fabricated molecular counts. Insulin effects vary by tissue; the case graph does not measure insulin.\n- The 80-minute source plan allocates only 12 minutes to CER and peer review; it cannot also complete the lengthy five-part final paper. The paper is preserved in a separate editable follow-up assessment, with conditional wording where it assumes practical results. Arrange supervised follow-up and teacher review of the rubric.\n'}
